{"actor":"model","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35269","model_call":26,"attempt":1,"model":"gpt-5.5","usage":{"input_tokens":13473,"output_tokens":217,"total_tokens":13690,"cached_tokens":12544,"reasoning_tokens":29},"output_characters":877,"decision":{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_880501965#4_1588757518","relevance":3,"reason":"This official-style Marshall Plan document directly states that, for the United States, the Marshall Plan provided markets for American goods and created reliable trading partners, in the context of Truman sending the plan to Congress and Congress approving it. This is direct evidence of how American leaders expected it to help American businesses.","learned":"A strong answer is that U.S. leaders expected the Marshall Plan to help American business by creating markets for American goods and reliable trading partners in a recovered Western Europe."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q1400","reason":"The congressional/Truman administration query is producing highly direct evidence about markets for American goods and policy approval; continue to find more grade-3 documents."}}}
{"actor":"runner","event":"state","topic_id":"2024-35269","model_calls":26,"document_judgments":21,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"request","topic_id":"2024-35269","model_call":26,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1400"},"model_calls":26,"document_judgments":21,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35269","model_call":26,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1400"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1400"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_10_219219617#5_416549493","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1400","rank":3,"snippet":"February 25, 1948 — the Economic Cooperation Act was resoundingly passed by a vote of 329 to 74. On April 3, 1948, President Truman signed the act that became known as the Marshall Plan. Participating countries included Austria, Belgium, Denmark, France, West Germany, Great Britain, Greece, Iceland, Italy, Luxembourg, the Netherlands, Norway, Sweden, Switzerland, and Turkey. Congress appropriated $13.3 billion during the life of the plan for European recovery. That aid provided much-needed capital and materials that enabled Europeans to rebuild the European continent’s economy. The <cover>Marshall Plan provided markets for American goods, created reliable trading partners, and supported the development of stable democratic governments in Western Europe. Congress’</cover>s approval of the Marshall Plan signaled an extension of the multilateralism of World War II into the postwar years. The plan was to terminate on June 30, 1952, with a possible 12-month extension. The plan was not a simple cash handover, but the temporary creation of an entire bureaucratic structure and extension of American government management in Europe. The generosity and commitment of the United States to its European allies during World War II, plus the Marshall Plan, made the European Union of today possible.\" ,"},"time":17},"model_calls":26,"document_judgments":21,"relevance_3":7,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
