{"actor":"server","event":"response","topic_id":"2024-35269","model_call":42,"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q1404"},"record":{"query":{"op":"next","qid":"q1404"},"response":{"burrow":"/data/ssd3/claclark/msm-2.1.burrow","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_30_1758403811#14_3722210436","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q1404","rank":5,"snippet":"7996.000000 , \"headings\": \" \" , \"segment\": \"The Korean War represented a time when the tensions underlying almost all international could flare into a full-scale conflict. Events like these firmly entrenched the concept of the World as the stage an immense conflict between the U.S. and the Soviet Union. Additionally, to the list of its security concerns the U.S. added the need to protect nations and economies abroad from the spread of communism, likely in order to protect <cover>markets and sources of raw materials for American manufacturers. Out of these concerns arose the Marshall Plan </cover>and Truman Doctrine, and ensuing policies, that sought to keep the capitalist flag waving, first in Western Europe, and then around the World. From this string of policies we get the legacy, however dark and regretful, of U.S. intervention in foreign nations and often supporting pro-American, if tyrannical regimes. Examples include the CIA ousting of Arbenz in Guatemala to protect land for the United Fruit Company and the CIA actions against Mossadegh in Iran that laid the groundwork for continuing conflict with modern-day Iran. Similarly, U.S. actions in ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_858866992#4_945817546","rank":4,"relevance":2}]},"time":9},"model_calls":42,"document_judgments":35,"relevance_3":17,"max_document_judgments":50,"max_model_calls":100}
